WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:00.990
<v Speaker 1>Let's get started.

2
00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:02.910
<v Speaker 2>Welcome.

3
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:16.590
<v Speaker 2>We're with the master and the disciple, Jafar ibn Mansour al-Yaman 's dialogue and the lofty edge of part 2, where the disciple asks at last about air, the unpaired element that mixes with everything and is encompassed by nothing.

4
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:22.270
<v Speaker 2>Air is the loftiest similitude, the sign that announces the failure of signs.

5
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:28.910
<v Speaker 1>And if you have been following the threads of this text with us, we are setting out on a very specific, deliberate mission here.

6
00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:29.630
<v Speaker 2>We really are.

7
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:35.550
<v Speaker 1>We have been mapping out this grand, just meticulously constructed architecture of creation.

8
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.710
<v Speaker 1>The scholar in this dialogue has been guiding his student through this massive system

9
00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.150
<v Speaker 1>built entirely on a catalog of worldly symbols.

10
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:48.910
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it's a framework where physical, tangible things in our cosmos point directly to spiritual entities.

11
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.910
<v Speaker 2>It is a brilliantly ordered system.

12
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:51.950
<v Speaker 1>Completely peer-to-peer.

13
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:52.590
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

14
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:59.390
<v Speaker 2>Everything in the lower physical cosmos has been neatly paired off to represent a specific rank of intermediary in the higher spiritual world.

15
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.710
<v Speaker 2>It provides this sense of total intellectual mastery for the disciple.

16
00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.070
<v Speaker 1>You look at the earth and the scholar explains that the earth points downward to one specific rank.

17
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.590
<v Speaker 1>Then you look at the islands and they point to another rank.

18
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.670
<v Speaker 2>The flowing rivers point to yet another.

19
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.790
<v Speaker 2>The disciple has been meticulously writing down this map.

20
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.150
<v Speaker 2>Everything has a place, you know.

21
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.550
<v Speaker 1>Everything has a counterpart.

22
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.470
<v Speaker 1>But as perfect as this architecture seems, there is a glaring omission.

23
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.870
<v Speaker 1>One element has been conspicuously absent from this peer-to-peer pointing.

24
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.990
<v Speaker 2>It's just been sitting there quietly since the very first concept of this teaching, waiting to be addressed.

25
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.190
<v Speaker 1>And the disciple is sharp.

26
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.750
<v Speaker 1>He has been closely following the master's map.

27
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.150
<v Speaker 1>and he finally spots the missing element.

28
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.910
<v Speaker 2>He looks at the system and sees that every other element in the cosmos has a pair.

29
00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.710
<v Speaker 2>Heaven has the earth, the sun has the moon, salty water has sweet water.

30
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.110
<v Speaker 1>And because they have peers, they can point downward to a rank of intermediaries.

31
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.350
<v Speaker 1>But air, the very first of the seven elements, is completely alone.

32
00:01:58.430 --> 00:01:59.550
<v Speaker 2>It is unpaired.

33
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.270
<v Speaker 2>It just encompasses everything else.

34
00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.030
<v Speaker 1>It mixes with everything, but nothing encompasses it.

35
00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.590
<v Speaker 1>So naturally the disciple asks about it.

36
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:12.950
<v Speaker 2>He has his notebook out, so to speak, and he wants to know exactly what air symbolizes in the catalog.

37
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.590
<v Speaker 2>He wants to finish the map.

38
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.030
<v Speaker 1>And that is our mission.

39
00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:24.670
<v Speaker 1>We are going to explore this final element, air, and understand exactly how it brings this entire system of symbols

40
00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.350
<v Speaker 1>to its absolute limit.

41
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.670
<v Speaker 2>But the scholar's response to the disciples' question is, well, it's a flat refusal.

42
00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.830
<v Speaker 2>He refuses to make air a symbol for any creation whatsoever.

43
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:35.790
<v Speaker 1>He just shuts it down.

44
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:36.750
<v Speaker 2>Completely.

45
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:44.990
<v Speaker 2>He tells the student that air is simply too exalted to point downward to any spiritual rank because absolutely nothing in creation matches it.

46
00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.270
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet.

47
00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:46.910
<v Speaker 1>No.

48
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.630
<v Speaker 1>I mean, why can't air just be slotted into the catalog of created symbols like the rest of them?

49
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.710
<v Speaker 1>If the heavens point to a rank and the earth points

50
00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:55.310
<v Speaker 1>to a rank.

51
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.630
<v Speaker 1>Why can't air just point to the highest created rank?

52
00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.110
<v Speaker 2>You're asking why break the system at the very top?

53
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:00.750
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

54
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:01.870
<v Speaker 1>Why break the pattern?

55
00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.870
<v Speaker 2>To answer that, the teacher doesn't just say no.

56
00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.790
<v Speaker 2>He knows the disciple needs to understand the mechanics of why it cannot be slotted in.

57
00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.230
<v Speaker 1>So he explains the scale.

58
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.110
<v Speaker 2>He does.

59
00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.430
<v Speaker 2>He presents a scale of magnitudes.

60
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:18.110
<v Speaker 2>He uses 3 nested comparisons to prove the absolute incomparability of air.

61
00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:23.310
<v Speaker 2>He tells the disciple, earth set against air is like a seal ring on a great plane.

62
00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.630
<v Speaker 1>Let's hold on to that and actually visualize it.

63
00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.750
<v Speaker 1>Think about a seal ring, just a tiny circular band of metal you would wear on your finger.

64
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:31.030
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

65
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:42.990
<v Speaker 1>If you drop that ring onto a great plain, a flat, endless expanse of land stretching out infinitely in every direction to the horizon, that single tiny ring is the entire earth.

66
00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.350
<v Speaker 1>All of our mountains, all of our oceans, just that speck of metal.

67
00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.310
<v Speaker 2>Yes, and the plane itself represents air.

68
00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.270
<v Speaker 2>It is not just big, it is a terrifying scale.

69
00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.550
<v Speaker 2>It dwarfs the earth so completely that the earth practically ceases to exist in comparison.

70
00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:58.590
<v Speaker 1>But he doesn't stop there.

71
00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.110
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't.

72
00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.270
<v Speaker 2>He moves to the second magnitude.

73
00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.590
<v Speaker 2>He says, atmosphere set against the heavens is like a seal ring in a vast desert.

74
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.950
<v Speaker 1>So the scale expands again.

75
00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:12.830
<v Speaker 1>We take the atmosphere, the very thing that just acted as the infinite plane surrounding the earth, and now we compare it to the heavens above.

76
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:16.190
<v Speaker 2>And suddenly the atmosphere itself is reduced to another tiny ring.

77
00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:19.270
<v Speaker 1>Completely lost in an even larger, unimagined

78
00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:19.950
<v Speaker 1>desert.

79
00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.590
<v Speaker 1>The mind is forced to keep zooming out further and further.

80
00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.910
<v Speaker 2>Finally, he delivers the third magnitude.

81
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:32.590
<v Speaker 2>The scholar says, heaven set against the full extent of air is like an egg on an immense plain.

82
00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:33.390
<v Speaker 1>Oh man.

83
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.470
<v Speaker 1>The scale is staggering.

84
00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.310
<v Speaker 1>You stack these magnitudes together.

85
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.030
<v Speaker 1>Earth is completely dwarfed by the atmosphere.

86
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.270
<v Speaker 2>The atmosphere is completely dwarfed by the heavens.

87
00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:51.950
<v Speaker 1>And the heavens themselves, the ultimate ceiling of our reality, are reduced to the size of a fragile little egg sitting on an immense plane when compared to the full extent of air.

88
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.030
<v Speaker 2>Air contains absolutely all of them.

89
00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:59.950
<v Speaker 2>Because of this absolute nested scale, the scholar explains that air cannot possibly point downward.

90
00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.990
<v Speaker 1>It cannot be slotted into a peer-to-peer system because it has no peer.

91
00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.870
<v Speaker 2>It can only point up one to the one who has no likeness, neither in the outward aspects nor the baton.

92
00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:11.470
<v Speaker 1>And since it encompasses everything else, there is no horizontal counterpart.

93
00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.870
<v Speaker 1>This inner dimension is completely singular.

94
00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:14.350
<v Speaker 2>It is.

95
00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:19.550
<v Speaker 2>But this revelation introduces a massive existential tension for the disciple.

96
00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.070
<v Speaker 2>He is a rigorous student and he knows his scripture.

97
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.190
<v Speaker 1>He really does.

98
00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:28.110
<v Speaker 1>And he brings up an entirely reasonable objection based on the text.

99
00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.750
<v Speaker 1>He cites chapter 16, verse 74.

100
00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:31.390
<v Speaker 2>Or says.

101
00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:35.630
<v Speaker 1>So do not strike likenesses for Allah, for he knows and you do not.

102
00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.510
<v Speaker 2>It is a strict, undeniable prohibition against making comparisons.

103
00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.190
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay to me.

104
00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.230
<v Speaker 1>Peter just spent all this time using air as a sign.

105
00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.470
<v Speaker 2>It does seem like a contradiction at first glance.

106
00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:52.990
<v Speaker 1>I mean, he just walked through three massive comparisons about seal rings and planes to make us understand air.

107
00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:58.470
<v Speaker 1>and that he claims he isn't violating a clear verse that strictly forbids striking likenesses.

108
00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.670
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it's a heavy accusation from the student.

109
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:06.510
<v Speaker 1>If you use a physical element to point to the Creator, aren't you by definition making a likeness?

110
00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:12.670
<v Speaker 2>Well, the scholar untangles this apparent contradiction by going back to the exact same chapter of the Quran.

111
00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.390
<v Speaker 2>He quotes chapter 16, verse 60.

112
00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:16.190
<v Speaker 1>Okay, let's hear it.

113
00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.910
<v Speaker 2>And Allah's is the loftiest similitude.

114
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:23.150
<v Speaker 1>So the disciple is looking at one verse, strictly forbidding likenesses,

115
00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.590
<v Speaker 1>And the scholar counters with another voice from the same chapter, affirming A loftiest similitude.

116
00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:29.710
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

117
00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.870
<v Speaker 2>How do those two exist at the same time?

118
00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:38.350
<v Speaker 2>The master's doctrine lies in a very careful, profound distinction between two totally different kinds of signs.

119
00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:39.710
<v Speaker 1>Okay, break that down for us.

120
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.310
<v Speaker 2>First, you have lesser signs.

121
00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.590
<v Speaker 2>These are forbidden as comparisons for the Creator.

122
00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:45.070
<v Speaker 2>Why?

123
00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:50.430
<v Speaker 2>Because if you use a lesser sign, you slot him into a pure relation alongside his creatures.

124
00:06:50.590 --> 00:07:01.150
<v Speaker 1>Meaning, if I say a flowing river points to a worldly guide, and then I say air points to the Creator, I have just made the Creator another item in a list of created things.

125
00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:05.870
<v Speaker 2>You've put the absolute ultimate reality on the same scale as a river or an island.

126
00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.110
<v Speaker 1>I have taken the most exalted and demoted him to just another rung on a ladder.

127
00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:10.830
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

128
00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:11.950
<v Speaker 2>To use a lesser

129
00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:13.630
<v Speaker 2>is to diminish the absolute.

130
00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:18.430
<v Speaker 2>But the loftiest similitude is licensed because it functions entirely differently.

131
00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:18.910
<v Speaker 1>How so?

132
00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.230
<v Speaker 2>It stands at the absolute top of the ladder.

133
00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.950
<v Speaker 2>It is completely incomparable to its own peers.

134
00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:29.150
<v Speaker 2>It cannot slot the creator into a pure relation because air itself has no equal in the physical cosmos.

135
00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:30.110
<v Speaker 1>I see.

136
00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.070
<v Speaker 1>The comparison demonstrates incomparability through scale.

137
00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:33.950
<v Speaker 2>It does.

138
00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.350
<v Speaker 2>It is crucial to understand that the scholar is not saying that air is like the creator.

139
00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:41.390
<v Speaker 2>He is saying that nothing in creation is like air.

140
00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:46.910
<v Speaker 1>And therefore, nothing, including air itself, can be like the one who created it.

141
00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:51.710
<v Speaker 2>This entire framework is grounded in the apophatic anchor of classical theology.

142
00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:56.110
<v Speaker 1>Understanding through negation, stripping away our human concepts.

143
00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:56.590
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

144
00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.350
<v Speaker 2>When we try to describe the absolute highest

145
00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:05.630
<v Speaker 2>reality, any positive word we use, like powerful or vast, is still a human word.

146
00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.670
<v Speaker 1>It secretly smuggles human limits into the description.

147
00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:09.390
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

148
00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:13.630
<v Speaker 2>So instead of asserting what the creator is, you rigorously define what he is not.

149
00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.590
<v Speaker 2>He's not measurable.

150
00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:16.590
<v Speaker 2>He is not bounded.

151
00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.910
<v Speaker 1>He is not a peer to anything we can observe.

152
00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.270
<v Speaker 2>This is specifically rooted in the chapter on consultation, verse 11.

153
00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.990
<v Speaker 2>It establishes definitively that no created predicate transfers cleanly to the creator.

154
00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.350
<v Speaker 1>This tension of knowing and not knowing.

155
00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.230
<v Speaker 1>is central to the entire journey.

156
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.870
<v Speaker 1>We see it perfectly captured in that famous sacred saying.

157
00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.550
<v Speaker 2>The one translated by William Chittick.

158
00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:36.110
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

159
00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:41.390
<v Speaker 1>William Chittick provided its most influential English translation in his book, The Sufi Path of Knowledge.

160
00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:48.590
<v Speaker 1>The saying goes, I was a hidden treasure and I loved to be known, so I created creation in order to be known.

161
00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:52.190
<v Speaker 2>That saying holds a profound, beautiful paradox.

162
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.510
<v Speaker 2>The entire created order, the earth, the rivers, the atmosphere, the heavens,

163
00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:00.510
<v Speaker 2>is a vast sign system addressed directly to a knower.

164
00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:06.830
<v Speaker 1>But the one being signed, the originator of all of it, remains completely hidden behind the signs.

165
00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.910
<v Speaker 2>Air is the loftiest similitude, the sign that announces the failure of signs.

166
00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:18.110
<v Speaker 1>It is the only sign at the absolute top of the cosmos whose very form insists that the signified is not on the same scale as the sign.

167
00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:23.310
<v Speaker 2>It points upward, and in the very act of pointing, it announces that it fails to capture what it points to.

168
00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.230
<v Speaker 1>So the disciple finally understands this.

169
00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.790
<v Speaker 1>He sees the massive ladder of signs they have built.

170
00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:32.190
<v Speaker 1>He sees the loftiest similitude standing at the very top encompassing everything else.

171
00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.430
<v Speaker 2>But human curiosity is relentless.

172
00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:34.990
<v Speaker 1>It is.

173
00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.430
<v Speaker 1>Having reached the top of the physical cosmos, he guides us one rung further.

174
00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.750
<v Speaker 1>He asks the ultimate terrifying question.

175
00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:43.310
<v Speaker 2>What stands between air and the creator?

176
00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:43.950
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

177
00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:45.790
<v Speaker 1>What is that final threshold?

178
00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:49.310
<v Speaker 2>The scholar answers him, but with extreme caution.

179
00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:52.750
<v Speaker 2>He names the absolute limit of the dialogue allor.

180
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:53.630
<v Speaker 1>The instance.

181
00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.590
<v Speaker 2>Yes, the instance, the doorway of divine will.

182
00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:59.790
<v Speaker 2>That originating moment is entirely unique.

183
00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:00.750
<v Speaker 1>Through it.

184
00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.150
<v Speaker 1>The originating command was commanded.

185
00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.550
<v Speaker 2>This is the exact boundary where the metaphor of error breaks down completely.

186
00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:13.390
<v Speaker 2>The master names this threshold, but he deliberately, carefully does not unfold it for the student.

187
00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:18.590
<v Speaker 1>Because this divine will is not something you can just map out like the islands or the rivers.

188
00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.510
<v Speaker 1>You cannot chart it on a piece of paper.

189
00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:21.470
<v Speaker 2>You really cannot.

190
00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:27.470
<v Speaker 2>We see the historical and theological weight of this in Paul Walker's study, Early Philosophical Shayism.

191
00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.790
<v Speaker 1>Oh yeah, he explains the Fatimid theological rigor on this

192
00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:31.710
<v Speaker 1>exact point.

193
00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:35.310
<v Speaker 2>The nature of this origination is beyond human categorization.

194
00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:41.790
<v Speaker 2>It is neither inside time the way ordinary moments are, nor is it outside time the way we usually imagine eternity.

195
00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.190
<v Speaker 1>It is the originating moment of time itself.

196
00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.190
<v Speaker 1>My brain is starting to knot up a bit just thinking about that.

197
00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:47.990
<v Speaker 2>It's meant to.

198
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:49.470
<v Speaker 1>You can't map that.

199
00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.990
<v Speaker 1>You can't draw a line from before to after if the concept of before doesn't even exist yet.

200
00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.950
<v Speaker 2>That is exactly the trap the disciple is facing.

201
00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:07.070
<v Speaker 2>To explain the origination of time using our human categories of before and after would be to fold it back into the created order it is supposed to proceed.

202
00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:10.510
<v Speaker 1>You would be using time to describe the creation of time.

203
00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.830
<v Speaker 2>It is a logical impossibility that shatters the intellect's ability to comprehend it.

204
00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.310
<v Speaker 1>So the discipline of silence must be honored.

205
00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:21.470
<v Speaker 1>The scholar names the boundary but refuses to dissect it.

206
00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:27.110
<v Speaker 2>And the disciple, standing at this metaphysical boundary, feels the sheer vertigo of what he is looking at.

207
00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.630
<v Speaker 1>He has reached the end of his intellectual map.

